\label{rem:2homog-comb}
Because $|G|$ is odd, $G$ contains no involution;  this is the single most important difference from the $2$-transitive case, and it forces every three-point stabilizer to have induced image in $\{1,C_3\}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] \emph{Triple systems.}  Each three-point orbit is already a $2$-\mbox{$(q,3,\lambda)$} design, $\lambda=1$ or $3$;  the $G$-invariant triple systems are the nonempty unions with $\lambda$ the corresponding subset sum \textup{(\textup{Proposition \ref{prop:union-orbits}}}, applied verbatim), and all of them have odd point stabilizers.  The Steiner systems arise exactly in the ranges of \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:2homog}}.
\item[(b)] \emph{Graphs.}  For any $2$-\mbox{$(q,3,\lambda)$} orbit, the incidence graph (points versus blocks) is biregular with valencies $(3,r)$, $r=\lambda(q-1)/2$, and the spectral results of \textup{\S\ref{sec:graphs}} apply verbatim;  by the criterion of \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:stblock}} the block graph is strongly regular whenever $\lambda=1$ and $q\ge9$ (the Steiner case), and the $\lambda\ge2$ rows are regular but generally not strongly regular, as in \textup{\S\ref{sec:graphs}}.
\item[(c)] \emph{Codes.}  The design code (point-row space over $\mathbb{F}_2$) and the constant-weight code are defined exactly as in \textup{\S\ref{sec:coding}};  since $r=\lambda(q-1)/2$, the self-orthogonality criterion of \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:descode}(iii)} is decided by the parities of $r$ and $\lambda$.
\item[(d)] \emph{Association schemes.}  The orbit partition is the class partition of a Schurian coherent configuration on triples \textup{(\textup{Proposition \ref{prop:valencies}})} with class number $\sigma_3$ of \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:2homog}};  because $G$ has no involution the configuration is not symmetric \textup{(generous transitivity on triples fails)}, in contrast with the $Sym(\mathcal{P})$ case.
\end{itemize}

\label{rem:2homog-general}
The same analysis applies to any $2$-homogeneous subgroup $G=V\rtimes M$ of $A\string\Gamma L(1,q)$ with $|G_0|$ odd;  by \textup{Lemma \ref{lem:2homog-class}} these are precisely all $2$-homogeneous non-$2$-transitive groups, and there is no almost simple example.  The square-subgroup case $M=(F_q^*)^2$ is \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:2homog}}.  If $M$ is enlarged by a field automorphism $\sigma\colon x\mapsto x^p$ of odd order $d$, the point stabilizer is $M\rtimes\langle\sigma\rangle$ of order $(q-1)d/2$ and the setwise stabilizer of a pair is $\langle\sigma\rangle$ \textup{(it fixes both points;  $-1
\notin M$ gives no transposition)}.  Then $\sigma$ fixes pointwise the $(p-2)$ points of $\mathbb{F}_p\setminus\{0,1\}$ and cyclically permutes the points of $\mathbb{F}_q\setminus\mathbb{F}_p$ in cycles of lengths dividing $d$, and the three-point orbits are obtained by fusing these pair-suborbits as in \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:2homog}};  the dichotomy $\lambda_X\in\{1,3\}$ persists, since $|G|$ remains odd.

\label{cor:blocktrans}
Let $\mathcal{T}=\mathcal{T}_I$ be a $G$-invariant simple triple system on $\mathcal{P}$, with $\varnothing
\ne I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,r\}$ \textup{(Propositions \ref{prop:union-orbits} and \ref{cor:all-invariant})}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $G$ is block-transitive on $\mathcal{T}$ if and only if $|I|=1$, i.e.\ $\mathcal{T}$ is a single orbit $\mathcal{T}_i$.
\item[(ii)] In particular, every Steiner triple system $2$-$(v,3,1)$ with a $2$-transitive automorphism group is block-transitive; equivalently, every Steiner row $\lambda=1$ of \textup{Table \ref{tab:main-lambda}} is a single orbit.
\item[(iii)] A union $\mathcal{T}_I$ with $|I|\ge2$ (hence $\lambda_I\ge2$) is not block-transitive.
\end{enumerate}

\label{cor:flagtrans}
Let $\mathcal{T}_i$ be a single orbit (hence block-transitive), with induced image $\overline{G}_{\mathcal{O}_i}\le S_3$ in the exact sequence of \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:main}(2)}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $G$ is flag-transitive on $\mathcal{T}_i$ if and only if $\overline{G}_{\mathcal{O}_i}$ acts transitively on the three points of $\mathcal{O}_i$, if and only if
\[
\overline{G}_{\mathcal{O}_i}\in\{C_3,S_3\};
\]
images $1$ and $C_2$ are not flag-transitive.
\item[(ii)] Reading the image column of \textup{Table \ref{tab:main}}, the classification is:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] \emph{All orbits flag-transitive:}  $PSL(n,q)$ with $n\ge3$ (both orbits, $S_3$, \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:psln}}); $Sp(2n,2)$ (both orbits, $S_3$, \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:sp2n-triples}}); $Alt(n)$/$Sym(n)$, the Mathieu groups, $HS$ and $Co_3$ (every orbit, $S_3$); and the projective overgroups $PGL(2,q)/P\string\Gamma L(2,q)$ (single orbit, $S_3$).
\item[(b)] \emph{Some orbits flag-transitive:}  $PSL(2,q)$: all three-point orbits are flag-transitive --- the single orbit for $q$ even ($S_3$) or $q\equiv3\pmod4$ ($C_3$), and the two orbits for $q\equiv1\pmod4$ ($S_3$); $PSU(3,q)$: the collinear orbit ($S_3$) and, for $q$ odd, the $\lambda=(q^2-1)/d$ orbits ($S_3$) and $\lambda=2(q^2-1)/d$ orbits ($C_3$), and for $q$ even the non-collinear merged orbits ($C_3$), are flag-transitive, while the $\lambda=6(q^2-1)/d$ orbits (image $1$) are not; $Ree(q)$: the $\lambda=2(q-1)$ orbits ($C_3$) and the two $\lambda=q-1$ orbits (collinear $C_3$, non-collinear $S_3$) are flag-transitive, while the $\lambda=6(q-1)$ (image $1$) and $\lambda=3(q-1)$ (image $C_2$) orbits are not; and on the affine side $AGL(1,q)$ ($\lambda=1$ with $S_3$, $\lambda=2$ with $C_3$) and $G_2'(q)$ (collinear $\lambda=2$ with $C_3$, hyperbolic and isotropic with $S_3$) are flag-transitive on those orbits, while their image-$1$ and image-$C_2$ collinear orbits are not.
\item[(c)] \emph{No orbit flag-transitive:}  $Sz(q)$: the circle orbit has image $C_2$ and every generic orbit image $1$.
\end{itemize}
The remaining affine families ($ASL(n,q)/AGL(n,q)$ for $n\ge2$, $ASp$, $A\string\Gamma L(1,q)$ and the Hering exceptions) are decided by the same criterion from the image column of \textup{Table \ref{tab:main}} and the affine theorems of \textup{Section \ref{sec:affine}}.  Every individual triple-orbit design $\mathcal{T}_i$ is block-transitive, and is flag-transitive exactly in the cases listed.  A nontrivial union $\mathcal{T}_I=\bigcup_{i\in I}\mathcal{T}_i$ with $|I|\ge2$ is \emph{not} block-transitive ($G$ preserves each constituent orbit, so no element of $G$ carries a block of $\mathcal{T}_i$ onto a block of $\mathcal{T}_j$ for distinct $i,j\in I$) and hence is not flag-transitive under $G$.  Consequently the only flag-transitive $G$-invariant triple systems on $\mathcal{P}$ are the flag-transitive single orbits themselves.
\end{enumerate}

\label{cor:arc-families}
The following orbit triple systems have $G$-arc-transitive incidence graphs:
\begin{enumerate}
\item every $3$-transitive family of \textup{Table \ref{tab:main}}, and every
$3$-homogeneous family whose triple stabilizer induces $C_3$ or $S_3$ on the
triple (unique orbit, $\lambda=v-2$);
\item every orbit of $PSL(2,q)$ on $q+1$ points, $q\equiv1\pmod4$ (both
orbits, $\lambda=(q-1)/2$), and the single orbit for $q$ even or
$q\equiv3\pmod4$;
\item an orbit of $AGL(1,q)$ on $q$ points is arc transitive if and only if
all blocks of its star at a point lie in a single ratio class (equivalently,
the stabilizer $G_a$ has a single orbit on the star); e.g.\ the $\lambda=2$
orbit for $q=7$ is arc transitive, while the $\lambda=3$ orbit for $q=7$ is
not ($2$ ratio classes, $2$ star orbits).
\end{enumerate}

\label{prop:arc-image}
Let $G$ be $2$-transitive on $\mathcal{P}$ and let $\mathcal{T}_i$ be a single orbit
triple system with representative $\mathcal{O}_i$.  For $\Delta\in\mathcal{T}_i$ write
\[
\overline{G}_{\Delta}=G_{\Delta}/G_{(\Delta)}\leSym(\Delta)\cong S_3
\]
for the induced image of the setwise stabilizer $G_{\Delta}$ modulo the
pointwise stabilizer $G_{(\Delta)}$;  throughout, an arc of the incidence
graph $\Gamma(\mathcal{T}_i)$ means an incident pair $(\alpha,X)$ with
$\alpha\in X\in\mathcal{T}_i$.  The following conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $G$ is transitive on the arcs of $\Gamma(\mathcal{T}_i)$;
\item[(ii)] $G$ is flag-transitive on $\mathcal{T}_i$;
\item[(iii)] $\overline{G}_{\mathcal{O}_i}$ is transitive on $\mathcal{O}_i$;
\item[(iv)] $\overline{G}_{\mathcal{O}_i}\cong C_3$ or $S_3$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular $\Gamma(\mathcal{T}_i)$ is $G$-arc transitive if and only if
$\overline{G}_{\mathcal{O}_i}\in\{C_3,S_3\}$.

\label{rem:arc-tt}
In the unitary rows the arc-transitivity data are consistent with, and refine,
the rank-$3$ permutation-character computations of \cite{tongviet2013}.

\label{rem:open}
The general orbit-to-design correspondence is complete for every fixed $2$-transitive permutation group \textup{(Theorem \ref{thm:main}(1)--(4)}\textup{)}.  For the family-specific data we distinguish four classes of statements:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Proved for all admissible parameters.}  The classical families \textup{(Sections \ref{sec:psl2}--\ref{sec:affine})}:  $PSL(2,q)$ and its projective overgroups, $PSL(n,q)$, $n\ge3$, on projective space, $Sp(2n,2)$, $Alt(n)$, the Mathieu groups (all $3$-transitive), $AGL(1,q)$, $A\string\Gamma L(1,q)$, the classical affine linear and symplectic rows, and the $G_2'(q)$ rows of \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:g2-classical}} \textup{(}$q=2^f\ge4$\textup{)};  together with the orbit numbers of $Sz(q)$ \textup{(Theorem \ref{thm:sz-orbits})}, of $Ree(q)$ \textup{(Theorem \ref{thm:ree-triples};  with the stabilizer distribution of \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:ree-structure}}}\textup{)}, of $PSU(3,q)$ \textup{(Theorem \ref{thm:main-psu}(3), closed forms in the $d=1$, the odd-$d=3$ \textup{(Proposition \ref{prop:psu-odd})} and the even-$d=3$ \textup{(Proposition \ref{prop:psu-even})} rows, with the small-$q$ stabilizer refinement of \textup{Proposition \ref{prop:psu-computed}}}\textup{)}, the Suzuki field extensions \textup{(Theorem \ref{thm:suzuki-field-final})} and the Ree field extensions for every field quotient $f'\mid 2m+1$:  cubic degree $f'=3$ by \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:ree-ext}} ($a=(q_0^2+2q_0+3)/2$, Lemma \ref{lem:ree-ext-cycles}), and $f'\ge5$ by the descent argument of \textup{Proposition \ref{prop:ree-descent}} ($a=(q_0^2+2q_0+3)/2$ for $3\mid f'$, $a=(q_0+3)^2/6$ for $3
\nmid f'$, Proposition \ref{prop:ree-ext-noncubic}), the diagonal overgroup orbit number $\sigma_3(PGU(3,q))$ \textup{(Proposition \ref{prop:psu-diag}, proved for every $q$)}\textup{)}, and the field conjugation of $P\string\Gamma U(3,q)$ (proved to cause no fusion for every $q\ge3$ by \textup{Proposition \ref{prop:psu-field-nofusion}}).
\item \textbf{Computed for an explicit range.}  The sporadic rows $HS$, $Co_3$ and $HS.2$ (degree $176$ resp.\ $276$), the Hering exceptions of degree $\le 729$ \textup{(including $3^6{:}\operatorname{SL}(2,13)$, \textup{Proposition \ref{prop:aff729}}\textup{)}}, the unitary stabilizer refinement of \textup{Proposition \ref{prop:psu-computed}} for $q\le49$ and the suborbit census of \textup{Table \ref{tab:psu-general}} for $q\le27$, the $Ree(27){:}3$ data of \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:ree-ext}}, and the $Sz(8){:}3$, $Sz(32){:}5$ data of \textup{Theorem \ref{thm:suzuki-field-final}};  the full field part $C_{2f}$ of $P\string\Gamma U(3,q)$ \textup{(generated by the Frobenius $\sigma_p$)} is likewise computed for these parameters, fusing certain $PGU(3,q)$-orbits for $q=8,9,25,27$ ($\sigma_3(P\string\Gamma U(3,q))=3,4,8,7$;  no closed form for general $q$ is proved, \textup{Remark~\ref{rem:psu-field}}).  Each such claim states its exact degree and group range \textup{(Chapter \ref{sec:verify})}.
\item \textbf{Conditional.}  The $P\string\Gamma U(3,q)$ full field part is computed for $q=8,9,25,27$ and is not claimed in closed form for every $q$ \textup{(Remark \ref{rem:psu-field};  it does not affect the rows of Table \ref{tab:main})};  the $P\string\Gamma U(3,q)$ conjugation is proved not to fuse for every $q$ by \textup{Proposition \ref{prop:psu-field-nofusion}}.  All other family statements of \textup{Table \ref{tab:main}}, including every Ree field extension, are unconditional.
\item \textbf{Not treated.}  The designs are not classified up to abstract isomorphism, and the specified group is not asserted to be the full automorphism group of each design.
\end{enumerate}
The finite computations of \textup{Chapter \ref{sec:verify}} verify the listed representations and do not replace a proof for arbitrary parameter values.
